GOTCHA (dark-factory, measured 2026-09-05, task 3588): `submit_task(planning_mode=True)` still COMBINES byte-identical submissions, so a dedupe test that files "the same task" twice can pass vacuously against a mechanism that is not the one under test.

MEASURED, on a real stack (SqliteTaskBackend + EventBuffer + TicketStore + real TaskInterceptor + create_mcp_server). Three submit_task calls with IDENTICAL title/description/prompt/metadata and planning_mode=True returned:
  {'task_id': '1', 'status': 'deferred', 'planning_mode': True}
  {'task_id': '1', 'status': 'deferred', 'combined': True, 'planning_mode': True}
  {'task_id': '1', 'status': 'deferred', 'combined': True, 'planning_mode': True}
— one row, `combined: True` on calls 2 and 3. Re-running with only the title/description/prompt VARIED (metadata unchanged) produced three distinct rows: task_id 1, 2, 3. So the collapse keys on the submission TEXT, not on metadata.

WHY THIS BITES. There is an existing norm memory (2026-06-15) recommending planning_mode=True precisely BECAUSE it is "synchronous, curator-bypassing" and avoids the curator COMBINING a new task into a semantically-adjacent one. That is still true of the curator's semantic combine — but it is not the whole story, and reading it as "planning_mode never combines" is what sets the trap. An exact-duplicate combine still applies on the planning_mode path.

CONSEQUENCE FOR TESTS. Any test that asserts "N filings produce exactly one row" must VARY the per-filing text, or it proves nothing about its own subject. In task 3588 this made a bounded-queue assertion for a new gate_subject dedupe guard pass while the guard was not yet wired — caught only because the guard's OTHER RED assertions failed in the same run and the pass looked wrong for the phase. Varying the text is also the more faithful reproduction: the real duplicate-carrier population it modelled (tasks 5902 -> 5916 -> 5929, all for subject 5879) each carried its own cycle's prose, and the metadata key was the only thing linking them.

PRACTICE: when a test's GREEN could be produced by pre-existing machinery rather than by the code under test, run it once against the un-wired tree and confirm it is RED. A test that is green in both phases is measuring something else.

