SUPERSEDES MEMORY 7cdc3c71 (task 7430) — read this one instead. That record says a tests/infra test ported to Python drops SILENTLY out of test_slot_timeout_marker.sh's deadline-capable roster (measured 144/0 -> 141/3, failing FC6b/F1/G3) and that any port must budget for teaching Sections F and G the Python shape. TRUE UNTIL 2026-09-19; CLOSED BY TASK 7626. Do not budget for that grammar work again.

CURRENT STATE. A `.py` is still NEVER a node — _f_node_list is unchanged, so the roster, D_ROSTER, Section G's G0 slice, the run-all-classification.manifest row and every doc reference stay keyed on the `.sh` basename. What was added is TEXT ATTRIBUTION: _f_strip_node appends a same-stem `.py` sibling's comment-stripped text to the node's own, gated on the `.sh` REALLY invoking it through an anchored python3/python verb — mere sibling existence is not enough, so a stray unrun `.py` stays inert. The direct predicate and every edge rule then see the sibling for free. Section G gained a parallel Python diversion dialect, selected from the scan target's file extension rather than from a hand-declared column.

A PORT MUST NOW EMIT THE DIALECT. This is a CONTRACT, not a free choice — a port that spells its invocation another way runs correctly and still drops out of one or both guards:
1. the wrapper really runs the sibling, through an anchored python3/python verb;
2. the nested invocation is an argv list headed by a QUOTED exec verb — `["bash", str(NESTED)]` — with the target either bound by a real assignment (`NESTED = SCRIPT_DIR / "test_x.sh"`; str() and os.fspath() both accepted) or written as a string literal in that same list. A list headed by a bare name (`[sys.executable, ...]`) is deliberately NOT an invocation, and neither is a DOCSTRING naming the suite in prose: `#`-stripping is shared with Python but does not remove a docstring, so prose is rejected by rule rather than by luck;
3. the spawn diverts stderr — stderr=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, capture_output=True, or stderr=subprocess.STDOUT PAIRED WITH a diverted stdout. Unlike bash the merge branch takes NO order test, because Python kwargs carry no ordering;
4. a multi-line spawn puts its closing paren on its own line. Section G stamps such a block from its body at a line beginning `)`; a paren trailing the last kwarg is never seen to close, and the fail-safe then counts the spawn as NOT captured — a false RED rather than a false green, but still a RED.

LANDED PROOF AND POINTERS: tests/infra/test_verify_env_ambient_isolation.{sh,py} (thin wrapper plus 540-line port). FC6b pins the derived route as via:test_occt_flock_gate.sh — not via:run_all.sh, which is what a prose-admitting grammar derives — and G3/G1 read the spawn at the .py :175-178 for 1 site / 0 unredirected. Guard 164 passed / 0 failed. Canonical doc: docs/notes/infra-test-bash-to-python-migration-policy.md, section "The Python dialect a port must emit"; that policy's Arm 2 (port a bash member when it is next touched for a flake) is ACTIVE as of task 7626.

Where a data.js node test goes (task 5834 split, 2026-10-14). The old 3,149-line dashboard/tests/js/data_poll.test.mjs is now five per-feature suites:
- data_poll: the poll loop (contract and auto-start, startPolling, in-flight guard, per-attempt deadline, backoff, jitter, df-data-refresh dispatch, 'Slowness pacing');
- data_apply: endpointsFor registry, applyKey, datumFor, keep-prior on failure;
- data_freshness: DF_DATA.__stale/__receipt publication;
- data_on_demand: ON_DEMAND_KEYS/requestOnDemand, REFRESH_OUTCOMES, onDemandView/onDemandDatum;
- data_scope: pollSetFor/scopePollingToTab and the rail badges.
Every suite loads data.js only through _data_loader.mjs::loadDataJs, which returns {api, window, events, fetchCalls, intervalCalls}; never copy it. Literals and stubs shared across suites (EXPECTED_ENDPOINT_COUNT, PLAIN_SPEC/DATUM_SPEC, CURATOR/COSTS/TASKS_PATH, TERMINAL_PROJECT, STALE_FAILURE_THRESHOLD, okResponse) live in _data_fixtures.mjs. Single-suite helpers stay in their suite.

