Completed: One builtin-name FAMILIES table: table-driven all-pairs disjointness (units.rs) + SPOT-bind the closed-world oracle's family list (unresolved_function.rs)
Description: Two halves of one SPOT fix: every compiler builtin-name family slice should be listed in ONE place, and both the disjointness tests and the closed-world builtin oracle's tests should derive from that list. Scope widened 2026-10-08 by Leo during the /unblock of #5476 (half B added; half A is the original task).

=== HALF A (original scope) — units.rs: replace the quadratic per-family disjointness tests ===
Discovered during the code-review amendment pass for task #5476 (placeholder-ratchet α). FILE: crates/reify-compiler/src/units.rs, the `mod tests` builtin-name-family disjointness tests; the newest is `flexure_ctor_fn_names_are_disjoint_from_other_families` (symbol-anchor; 17 `*disjoint*` test fns in units.rs as of 2026-10-08).

OBSERVED (measured): `flexure_ctor_fn_names_are_disjoint_from_other_families` is ~26 near-identical `assert!(!SIBLING_SLICE.contains(name), "...")` blocks after #5476's step-17 widened it, and it is the Nth such test in the file. The file convention (recorded in the `PARSE_FN_NAMES` amendment comment in units.rs) is that the NEWEST family checks itself against ALL existing siblings in one new test, leaving older sibling tests unedited. Cost is quadratic in the number of families, and coverage is only newest-vs-predecessors, not all-pairs.

PROPOSED FIX (reviewer's, not yet validated): `const FAMILIES: &[(&str, &[&str])]` listing every builtin-name family slice with its display name, and one all-pairs test:

    for (i, (an, a)) in FAMILIES.iter().enumerate() {
        for (bn, b) in &FAMILIES[i + 1..] {
            for n in *a {
                assert!(!b.contains(n), "{n:?} appears in both {an} and {bn}");
            }
        }
    }

WHY DISJOINTNESS MATTERS (do not delete these tests): the `NoUserFunctions` builtin-typing ladder in crates/reify-compiler/src/expr.rs dispatches by name through a chain of `is_*_typed_fn` arms. Disjointness is what makes each arm's POSITION in that ladder unobservable; a name in two families is silently shadowed by the earlier arm (a silent mis-typing, not a compile error).

=== HALF B (added 2026-10-08) — unresolved_function.rs: the oracle's union and its test table are two hand-kept lists ===
FILE: crates/reify-compiler/src/unresolved_function.rs. `pub fn is_known_builtin` ORs ~20 family slices by hand (`GEOMETRY_QUERY_HELPER_NAMES.contains(&name) || … || FLEXURE_CTOR_FN_NAMES.contains(&name) || …`). The test module keeps a SEPARATE hand-written `const ALL_FAMILY_SLICES: &[(&str, &[&str])]`, which drives `is_known_builtin_recognises_every_compiler_family` and `eval_deferred_names_are_disjoint_from_every_registered_family`.

DEFECT (heuristic 11 SPOT; found by the fresh-agent review of #5476 steps 15-18, verified by reading the file): if someone deletes a family's row from `ALL_FAMILY_SLICES` (e.g. the `("FLEXURE_CTOR_FN_NAMES", …)` row #5476 added), NO test reds. Both tests silently stop covering that family, and the manifest-disjointness guard goes blind to it. Conversely, a new family added to `is_known_builtin` but not to the table is never checked against `EVAL_DEFERRED_BUILTIN_NAMES`. Pre-existing on main for every family; #5476 followed the pattern.

FIX DIRECTION: make the family list a single source both modules consume. For example, a crate-visible `BUILTIN_NAME_FAMILIES: &[(&str, &[&str])]` that `is_known_builtin` iterates for its slice arms (keeping any non-slice arms, e.g. the registry/predicate ones, explicit), with units.rs's FAMILIES (half A) and unresolved_function.rs's `ALL_FAMILY_SLICES` both being that same table. At minimum, a test that fails when the union and the table disagree. The architect should choose where the table lives: units.rs vs unresolved_function.rs vs a small new module, keeping the dependency direction sane.

